# A BARE NAME IS THE STANDARD LIBRARY, ALWAYS. `io.zg` sits beside this file and is a module of
# this project, and `import "io"` still names the standard library's — the prefix decides the
# root and nothing on disk can change that (docs/runtime/package.md).
#
# Both are here, under the two spellings that tell them apart. If the bare name had picked the
# neighbour up, `io.println` would not exist and this would not build.
import (
	"io"

	"./io" as mine
)

fn main() {
	io.println("the standard library")
	io.println(mine.mark())
}
